= I/ INTRODUCTION

The rapid growth of artificial intelligence (AI), the Internet of Things (IoT), and big data has created a substantial demand for energy-efficient computing hardware @wang_recent_2020. While training large-scale deep learning models consumes substantial electrical energy @strubell_energy_2019, the biological human brain operates at a low average power consumption of approximately 20 W @laughlin_energy_2001. This discrepancy highlights the limitations of the conventional von Neumann architecture, which physically separates the processing unit (CPU/GPU) from the memory unit @li_review_2018. Continuous data transport across a bandwidth-limited bus creates the "von Neumann bottleneck," accounting for up to 80% of the total energy dissipation in modern processors @sze_efficient_2017. To overcome this barrier, in-memory computing (IMC) and neuromorphic architectures have emerged as paradigms where computations are executed directly within the memory array @yunUnravelingRolePolydopamines2022. Among the candidates for IMC hardware, the memristor is a two-terminal device whose conductance can be dynamically modulated by the history of applied electrical stimulation @wang_recent_2020. The device was first postulated by Leon Chua in 1971 @chua_memristor_1971 and experimentally realized in 2008 by HP Labs using a metal/insulator/metal thin-film crossbar structure @strukov_missing_2008.

Although conventional inorganic memristors, such as metal oxides, offer high switching speeds and density, they suffer from limitations including lattice degradation and high forming voltages @li_review_2018. Consequently, organic semiconductors are increasingly investigated due to their low-energy ion transport, inherent biocompatibility, and environmentally benign synthesis @yunUnravelingRolePolydopamines2022. A promising bio-inspired organic polymer is PDA, a synthetic eumelanin-like material that can be deposited via simple electropolymerization @varolElectropolymerizationPolydopamineElectrodeSupported2023. At the molecular level, the PDA matrix is a heterogeneous mixture of cyclized 5,6-dihydroxyindole (DHI) and uncyclized amine chains @alfieri_chemistry_2018, held together by a combination of covalent linkages and non-covalent forces @alfieri_chemistry_2018. The PDA matrix contains a high density of active functional groups, including catechol (reduced state, containing hydroxyl -OH groups) and quinone (oxidized state, containing carbonyl C=O groups) @lee_polydopamine_2020. The interplay between these amine, imine, and phenolic groups imparts PDA with a zwitterionic nature, allowing its surface charge and internal electronic structure to be tuned through redox reactions or protonation @yu_pdop_2010 @zhouNaturalBiomaterialbasedMemristor2022, and these same functional groups dictate the charge transport behavior of the material @gao_ion_2014. In a hydrated or structurally intact PDA matrix, electrical conduction is not mediated solely by electronic drift; instead, it is governed by PCET, a concerted process where electronic conduction through the conjugated $pi$-systems is coupled with protonic conduction @hiruma_bio-inspired_2019. Unlike bulk ion diffusion where atoms physically migrate through a lattice, protonic conduction in hydrated polymer systems occurs via the Grotthuss mechanism, where protons hop along hydrogen-bonded networks. The catechol and amine groups form a dense, continuous hydrogen-bonded network @lee_polydopamine_2020, in which, under an applied electric field, a proton binds to one functional group while another proton is released to an adjacent group, allowing rapid charge transport across the polymer without the physical mass transport of a single atomic unit @hiruma_bio-inspired_2019. Modulating the ratio of catechol to quinone states allows the engineering of proton-hopping sites and free radical density @zhouNaturalBiomaterialbasedMemristor2022, where enhancing protonation, for instance, leads to closer lattice packing and lower free radical density, suppressing OFF-state leakage and providing a controlled medium for ion permeation @zhouNaturalBiomaterialbasedMemristor2022.

Depending on the retention characteristics of their conductance states, resistive switching devices are broadly classified into non-volatile and volatile systems @wang_recent_2020. In PDA-based non-volatile memory (NVM) devices, the mechanism has been attributed to oxygen-vacancy-mediated filamentary switching, where charge trapping in localized states provides stable retention for long-term plasticity after the external bias is removed @yunUnravelingRolePolydopamines2022. For example, an Al/PDA-PPy3Ir/ITO flexible memory device achieved a turn-on voltage of -1 V and an ON/OFF current ratio of $6.3 times 10^3$ @gongInSituSurfaceModification2022. Previous research has shown that treating PDA with acid (protonation via 12 M HCl) enhances its structural packing and diminishes free radicals, increasing the ON/OFF ratio of an FTO/PDA/Ag memristor from $10^2.5$ to $10^4.4$ while maintaining thermal stability up to 100°C @zhouNaturalBiomaterialbasedMemristor2022. However, although non-volatile devices exhibit stable retention, resetting them remains challenging due to the stochastic nature of filament rupture @nibhanupudiUltrafastSwitchingMemristors2024.

In contrast to NVMs, volatile memristors (or threshold switches) exhibit transient conductance states that spontaneously decay back to the HRS when the applied voltage drops below a specific holding threshold voltage, which mimics biological synapses for implementing STP, PPF, and integrate-and-fire artificial neuron spiking @wang_recent_2020. An electropolymerized Ag/PDA/Au volatile memristor exhibited an threshold voltage of less than 0.3 V, a thin active layer of 16 nm, and energy consumption of 10 pJ per spike with operating frequencies between 4 and 75 Hz @zhongElectropolymerizedDopaminebasedMemristors2025. Similarly, photochromic transient devices utilizing BPQDs\@PDA-PSP have demonstrated ON/OFF ratios of $1.02 times 10^4$ with turn-on voltages of -1.52 V @zhao_black_2024. This threshold switching relies on transient, field-driven ionic migration, where the resulting nanoscale filaments are unstable due to the Gibbs-Thomson effect (characterized by a high surface-to-volume ratio and elevated interfacial energy) @midya_anatomy_2017, such that once the electric field is removed, thermodynamic back-diffusion acts as a restoring force, spontaneously rupturing the pathway and returning the device to the HRS @midya_anatomy_2017. However, these transient filaments exhibit instability in organic matrices, leading to limited cycle endurance, molecular degradation, and premature device failure under continuous operation @zhouNaturalBiomaterialbasedMemristor2022.

To overcome the inherent instability of organic volatile memristors, this thesis presents a biomimetic Cu/C/PDA/ITO crosspoint memristor that addresses this instability through two engineering modifications @lee_polydopamine_2020, including electrochemical proton-doping to achieve the protonated state and a carbon barrier layer to control copper ion injection @zhao_breaking_2018. By utilizing the PCET mechanism within this stabilized protonated network, this architecture aims to achieve reliable volatile threshold switching capable of sustaining $10^6$ cycles to emulate STP dynamics @zhouNaturalBiomaterialbasedMemristor2022.
